# Kuramoto phase oscillators — manifold's first demo on the prism substrate.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# N phase oscillators couple through a SHARED LINK and synchronize. This is
# `../manifold`'s Kuramoto model expressed as a process-bigraph — the first
# proof that "an adaptive / dynamical network is a prism theory, not new
# engine" (docs/categorical-core.md §9, the M5 manifold domain). It reuses,
# unchanged, the exact mechanisms that run grow-divide-glucose:
#
#   • each oscillator's phase θ is an ADDITIVE scalar on its OWN node — it
#     accumulates dθ each tick, exactly like a cell's mass accumulates growth;
#   • the coupling is a MEAN FIELD on a shared link `field` — the running sum
#     Σⱼ [cosθⱼ, sinθⱼ]. Each oscillator emits the DELTA of its own phase-vector
#     and the engine's ADDITIVE fold on `array[[2]]` accumulates them, so the
#     link always holds the current sum (the same Δposition model the spatial
#     movers use: the additive fold IS the reduction — no new primitive). This
#     is field↔glucose, θ↔mass: a shared pool read fresh each tick + own state.
#
# The order parameter R = |field| / N measures coherence. All oscillators start
# in phase (θ=0, so the pool seeds at [N,0], R=1) but have SPREAD natural
# frequencies ω. With NO coupling (k=0) they drift apart and R decays toward 0;
# with strong coupling (k=4) they entrain and R stays near 1. Two convergences
# stack (categorical-core.md §5): `field` is a shared replica (→ a `mesh:` link
# in Demo 2) and synchronization is the dynamical attractor reading it.
# Asserted in crates/chrysalis/tests/kuramoto_sync.rs.
#
# Run: `chrysalis run kuramoto.ys --time 20`   (strong coupling by default)

# One oscillator's dynamics. Read the mean-field sum [sx,sy] and own phase θ;
# advance θ by ω plus the Kuramoto pull toward the mean field; emit (dθ, the Δ
# of [cosθ,sinθ]). The mean-field pull K·R·sin(Ψ−θ) is, with the raw sum
# [sx,sy] = N·R·[cosΨ,sinΨ], exactly (K/N)·(sy·cosθ − sx·sinθ) — and the self
# term cancels (sinθ·cosθ − cosθ·sinθ = 0), so summing over ALL oscillators
# (the whole pool, self included) is correct. `dt` is the integration step —
# small and fixed for Euler stability, and DECOUPLED from the engine tick (the
# per-oscillator composite ticks at the default rate, so a self-wired `interval`
# would be overridden too coarse; first-class multi-scale composite intervals
# are task #70). `interval` stays only so the engine schedules the Spin.
process Spin[omega :: Float = 1.0, k :: Float = 4.0, n :: Float = 8.0, dt :: Float = 0.05]
  ~{theta :: Float, field :: array[[2], float], interval :: Float = 1.0}
  ->{theta :: Float, field :: array[[2], float]}
(
  sx       = field.at(0) |
  sy       = field.at(1) |
  c        = theta.cos() |
  s        = theta.sin() |
  coupling = (k / n) * (sy * c - sx * s) |
  dtheta   = (omega + coupling) * dt |
  next     = theta + dtheta |
  {theta: dtheta, field: [next.cos() - c, next.sin() - s]}
)

# An oscillator carries its own phase θ and attaches to the shared mean-field
# link (mirror of grow-divide-glucose's Cell: θ↔mass, field↔glucose).
composite Oscillator[theta0 :: Float = 0.0, omega :: Float = 1.0, k :: Float = 4.0, n :: Float = 8.0]
  ~{field :: array[[2], float] @ field = [0.0, 0.0]}
  ->{theta :: Float @ theta, field :: array[[2], float] @ field}
(
  theta: theta0 |
  field: field |
  spin: Spin[omega: omega, k: k, n: n] ~{theta: theta, field: field} ->{theta: theta, field: field}
)

# A tile of N=8 oscillators on ONE shared mean-field link. All start in phase
# (θ=0) so the pool seeds at [N,0]; `omegas` gives each a different natural
# frequency (the disorder pressure coupling must overcome). `field` (the pool)
# is exposed so the test reads the order parameter R = |field| / N.
composite Tile[
  omegas :: map[Float] = {
    '0': 0.6, '1': 0.7, '2': 0.8, '3': 0.9,
    '4': 1.1, '5': 1.2, '6': 1.3, '7': 1.4
  },
  k :: Float = 4.0,
  n :: Float = 8.0,
  field0 :: array[[2], float] = [8.0, 0.0]
]
  ->{field :: array[[2], float] @ field, oscillators :: map[Oscillator] @ oscillators}
(
  # the pool seeds at Σ of the initial phase-vectors = [N, 0] (all θ0 = 0)
  link field :: array[[2], float] = field0 |
  oscillators: {
    # '{id}' is a COMPUTED (interpolated) key — a bare `id:` is a literal field
    # name, which would collapse every oscillator into one entry keyed "id".
    '{id}': Oscillator[theta0: 0.0, omega: w, k: k, n: n] ~{field: ~field} ->{field: ~field}
    for id, w in omegas
  }
)
